 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Sensing principle",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The SR MOSFET's own RDS(on) serves as the current shunt. The comparator reads VDS between VD and VS: when current starts in the body diode VDS falls past the turn-on threshold VTH2 (-150 to -50 mV) and the gate is driven high; as the current decays VDS rises through the turn-off threshold VTH1 (a few to about 20 mV below zero) and the gate is pulled low. VTH3 is a positive VDS threshold (2.5 V typ at VCC = 10 V). Blanking logic lets it work in CCM, DCM and CrCM."
  },
  {
   "topic": "UVLO and sleep",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Until VCC rises past VCC ON (10.55 V typ) the gate driver is off and the chip draws only its start-up current (200 µA max). Falling below VCC UVLO (9 V typ) from any state returns it to this mode. Pulling EN below about 1.6 V puts it to sleep, with the gate off and the supply current at its sleep value."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Normal mode and synchronised enable",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Normal mode starts once VCC is above the turn-on threshold and EN is above VENHI. On entry from UVLO the gate stays low until VDS has once exceeded VTH3, so the driver never starts partway through a conduction interval, which with the minimum on time could otherwise force reverse current. After that one event it switches every cycle. Flyback and LLC converters use the synchronised enable alike."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Minimum on time (MOT)",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Once the gate turns on, VDS collapses with ringing that could trip the comparator into turning off. During the MOT the comparator is ignored, so the gate stays high for at least the programmed time, 200 ns to 3 µs typ set by RMOT (5 kΩ to 75 kΩ). The MOT also sets the shortest SR conduction time and so limits the largest duty cycle of the primary switch."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Turn-off and blanking, DCM and CrCM",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The gate stays on until the falling current brings VDS up to VTH1. In DCM the current crosses slowly; after turn-off the residual current flows in the body diode and VDS jumps negative again, so VTH2 is blanked for tBLANK (17 µs typ, set inside) after VTH1 trips. The blanking ends early as soon as VDS rises past VTH3, readying the chip for the next cycle."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Turn-off in CCM",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "In CCM the primary switch turning back on drives the SR current down steeply; the fast VTH1 crossing turns the gate off, and a quick turn-off matters, both to prevent shoot-through with the primary and to cut switching loss. The blanking period still follows and is reset when VDS passes VTH3."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Resonant half-bridge",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "In an LLC half-bridge each SR switch is handled as in the flyback, the difference being that its current is a half sine. Each secondary leg has its own controller and MOSFET."
  },
  {
   "topic": "MOT protection mode",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "If the VDS sensed at the end of the minimum on time is already above VTH1, meaning the conduction lasted less than the MOT, the next gate pulse is suppressed. This avoids reverse current at very low duty cycle or light and no load, and lowers standby power. The check runs every cycle in both normal and protection modes, so normal switching returns by itself once the conduction time again exceeds the MOT."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Choosing OVT",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "OVT shifts the turn-off threshold VTH1: tied to GND -3.5 mV typ, open -9.5 mV typ, tied to VCC -18 mV typ (VS = 0 V). The three ranges suit MOSFETs of different on-resistance."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Enable pulse widths",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "EN is a slow on/off control with thresholds VENHI and VENLO. A high pulse must last over 20 µs, timed between its rising and falling VENHI crossings; a low pulse must last over 10 µs, timed between its falling and rising VENLO crossings."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Bypass VCC with a ceramic capacitor right at the chip. Run VD as a high-voltage trace to the MOSFET drain with no added filter or series limiting. Take VS as a Kelvin tap at the MOSFET's source pin, separate from GND (pin 7). Make the gate loop tight and its area small; a small series gate resistor is advised, especially with MOSFETs in parallel."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching timing",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "Turn-on delay tDon runs from VDS crossing VTH2 to the gate rising; turn-off delay tDoff from VDS crossing VTH1 to the gate falling. Rise and fall times are taken between 10 % and 90 % of the gate swing."
  }
 ],
